detail.abstract:
Schedule A litigation is a popular and controversial litigation strategy that allows brand owners to obtain a temporary restraining order (TRO) freezing hundreds of online accounts suspected of selling counterfeit goods. The disabling effects of the TRO force online merchants, most of them based in China, to submit to settlements or to suffer default judgments.&nbsp; 
<br>
<br>
While swiftly achieving the desired effect of disabling suspect accounts, Schedule A litigation does not prevent defendants from opening new accounts using fictitious identities. Defendants are able to open new accounts in short order and to resume their illegal activities. This possibility limits the effectiveness of Schedule A litigation.&nbsp;
<br>
<br>
Online merchants based in China are able to open new accounts despite the TRO due to their ability to easily evade identity verification procedures on e-commerce platforms, such as Amazon. This Article sets forth procedures that e-commerce platforms should use to accurately verify the true names and identities of online merchants based in China. Accurate verification will prevent defendants subject to Schedule A injunctions from opening new accounts using false identities and improve the effectiveness of Schedule A litigation.&nbsp;
